WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.198
Before the lens goes into your camera, try something.

2
00:00:03.598 --> 00:00:04.938
Put this in its place.

3
00:00:04.938 --> 00:00:08.897
A thin disc with a single hole, a quarter of a millimetre across.

4
00:00:09.137 --> 00:00:09.753
No glass.

5
00:00:09.753 --> 00:00:11.259
Nothing to focus with.

6
00:00:11.709 --> 00:00:13.795
Open the shutter, count, and close it.

7
00:00:13.795 --> 00:00:15.662
And the film has caught a picture.

8
00:00:16.462 --> 00:00:17.745
So here's the question.

9
00:00:17.745 --> 00:00:19.418
How can a hole make a picture?

10
00:00:21.533 --> 00:00:24.826
Most people guess that the hole acts like a tiny lens.

11
00:00:24.826 --> 00:00:27.997
That it bends the light, and squeezes it into focus.

12
00:00:28.497 --> 00:00:29.550
It's a good guess.

13
00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:30.486
It's also wrong.

14
00:00:30.986 --> 00:00:31.921
A hole is a gap.

15
00:00:31.921 --> 00:00:35.487
There's no glass in it, and nothing to bend light to a focus.

16
00:00:36.087 --> 00:00:37.073
So what does it do?

17
00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:40.661
Start with no camera at all.

18
00:00:40.661 --> 00:00:43.806
Just a tree, and a sheet of film, out in the open.

19
00:00:44.256 --> 00:00:47.462
Every point on the tree scatters light in every direction.

20
00:00:47.862 --> 00:00:51.253
So light from the top of the tree lands all over the film.

21
00:00:51.253 --> 00:00:53.241
And so does light from the bottom.

22
00:00:53.641 --> 00:00:57.349
Every spot on the film gets light from every part of the tree.

23
00:00:57.349 --> 00:00:59.742
Add it all up, and you get an even glow.

24
00:01:00.242 --> 00:01:02.244
The light for a picture is all there.

25
00:01:02.244 --> 00:01:03.705
It's just completely mixed.

26
00:01:04.983 --> 00:01:07.874
Now put a wall in the way, with one small hole in it.

27
00:01:08.474 --> 00:01:09.941
Before I show you, decide.

28
00:01:09.941 --> 00:01:11.352
What happens on the film?

29
00:01:14.792 --> 00:01:20.028
From the top of the tree, the wall stops every ray except the one lined up with the hole.

30
00:01:20.028 --> 00:01:23.440
That ray goes straight through, and lands low on the film.

31
00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:27.088
From the bottom of the tree, one ray gets through as well.

32
00:01:27.088 --> 00:01:27.860
It lands high.

33
00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:31.844
Now each part of the tree has its own spot on the film.

34
00:01:31.844 --> 00:01:32.984
That is a picture.

35
00:01:32.984 --> 00:01:36.024
Upside down, because the rays cross at the hole.

36
00:01:36.724 --> 00:01:38.444
And notice what the hole did.

37
00:01:38.444 --> 00:01:39.451
It added nothing.

38
00:01:39.451 --> 00:01:43.246
It took away almost all of the light, and what's left is sorted.

39
00:01:45.267 --> 00:01:47.614
If that's really how it works, you can test it.

40
00:01:48.014 --> 00:01:51.177
About a thousand years ago, Ibn al-Haytham did.

41
00:01:51.177 --> 00:01:55.822
Several lamps in a row, one small opening, and a dark room behind it.

42
00:01:56.322 --> 00:02:00.773
If light goes straight through the opening, how many spots land on the far wall?

43
00:02:00.773 --> 00:02:01.775
And in what order?

44
00:02:05.015 --> 00:02:07.968
One spot for each lamp, in the opposite order.

45
00:02:07.968 --> 00:02:10.792
Cover one lamp, and only its spot goes dark.

46
00:02:11.392 --> 00:02:14.362
Each lamp's light keeps to its own straight line.

47
00:02:14.362 --> 00:02:16.363
Nothing mixes, and nothing bends.

48
00:02:18.167 --> 00:02:20.268
So why isn't every camera just a hole?

49
00:02:20.268 --> 00:02:22.479
Because of a trade you can't get out of.

50
00:02:22.979 --> 00:02:25.685
Make the hole bigger, and more light gets in.

51
00:02:25.685 --> 00:02:30.435
But now each point of the tree paints a blob, not a dot, and the blobs overlap.

52
00:02:30.435 --> 00:02:31.819
Brighter, and blurrier.

53
00:02:32.319 --> 00:02:34.693
Make it smaller, and the picture sharpens.

54
00:02:34.693 --> 00:02:35.484
Up to a point.

55
00:02:35.984 --> 00:02:40.475
Squeeze light through a tiny enough hole, and it spreads out on the far side.

56
00:02:40.475 --> 00:02:42.109
The picture goes soft again.

57
00:02:42.709 --> 00:02:44.476
So there's a best size.

58
00:02:44.476 --> 00:02:48.240
For this camera it works out at 0.24 millimetres.

59
00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:51.313
The disc in your kit is drilled at 0.25.

60
00:02:53.133 --> 00:02:54.877
And here's what that costs.

61
00:02:54.877 --> 00:03:00.172
The smallest opening your camera's lens ever uses is about two millimetres across.

62
00:03:00.172 --> 00:03:03.337
The pinhole lets in sixty-seven times less light.

63
00:03:03.937 --> 00:03:05.561
So you don't click the shutter.

64
00:03:05.561 --> 00:03:06.976
You open it, and you count.

65
00:03:07.476 --> 00:03:09.165
In full sun: open, shut.

66
00:03:09.165 --> 00:03:11.207
In the shade, count to three.

67
00:03:11.207 --> 00:03:13.741
Indoors by a window, count to forty.

68
00:03:15.667 --> 00:03:18.081
That's the pinhole experiment in your kit.

69
00:03:18.081 --> 00:03:20.554
Two pictures, made with nothing but a hole.

70
00:03:21.154 --> 00:03:23.067
But it leaves you with a problem.

71
00:03:23.067 --> 00:03:26.545
A sharp picture needs a small hole, and a small hole is dim.

72
00:03:27.045 --> 00:03:31.120
What if you could keep the big, bright opening, and get the sharp picture too?

73
00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:34.395
That's the job of the piece that goes in next.

74
00:03:34.395 --> 00:03:34.899
The lens.

